{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F11 is the source's foundation-to-law account for change entry, classification and reuse, ambiguity governance, and shared-interface migration. Its additional instance premises are changing specifications during work, heterogeneous reports entering one product, shared interfaces with multiple producers and consumers, and decisions governed by policy or reserved human authority. The source calls P0 recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6 independent; P4b derived from P0 and P2 under non-reacquirability of step-produced facts; and P5 derived from P0 and P6 under an open-world premise. These are assertions about the subject's foundations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 788,
          "quote": "Its instance premises, which the commitments do not supply, are",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20change%20management%20and%20ambiguity",
          "start_line": 775
        }
      ],
      "key": "foundation_to_law_change_family",
      "label": "Foundation-to-law change and ambiguity family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The common entry operation for substantive reports, including bugs, feature requests, issues, regressions, operator findings, release blockers and scenario failures. It determines substantiveness, product boundary and scope, lawful class, constitutional re-entry point, affected downstream surfaces and evidence requiring repricing/rederivation/reproof, and release-scope retention or repricing. Its evidence selection is S_Δ over impact cover I(Δ). Diagnosis distinguishes defective realisation, inadequate requirement or design, inaccurate predictive model, and insufficient observation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 854,
          "quote": "There is one front door for substantive change.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FUniversal%20intake%20triage",
          "start_line": 827
        }
      ],
      "key": "universal_intake_triage",
      "label": "Universal intake triage"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Minimum declared change states with re-entry and downstream flow: goal_reprice changes bounded work-wave focus, enters Goals, and flows through Intent, Product, Requirements, Design, Code and Evidence where deeper constitutional truth changes; intent_reprice changes direction or scope, enters Intent, and flows through Product, Requirements, Design, Code and Evidence; product_reprice changes current product realisation while intent stays stable, enters Product, and flows through Requirements, Design, Code and Evidence; requirement_reprice changes constitutional truth while direction stays stable, enters Requirements, and flows through Design, Code and Evidence; design_reframe changes realisation structure while active intent and requirement truth stay stable, enters Design, and flows through Code and Evidence; realization_refactor changes local code, configuration or attributes without intended constitutional or structural change, enters only the realised surface, and flows nowhere upstream.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 888,
          "quote": "The minimum lawful change classes, each with its re-entry point and the",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FChange%20classes",
          "start_line": 878
        }
      ],
      "key": "change_classes",
      "label": "Declared change classes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One reusable intake classification, annotated C for reproducible facts and declared-rule application, J for residual semantic or materiality judgment, and O for rulings reserved to their actual owner. Its ordered components are authority traversal, public delta, accepted-design coverage and rule-determined treatment. Relation states changed/unchanged/unknown, identity additions/removals/retained changed values, and coverage states covered/missing/stale/ambiguous/outside-scope are computed facts, not evaluation results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 975,
          "quote": "C denotes reproducible facts and declared rule\napplication; J denotes residual semantic or materiality judgment; O denotes\na ruling reserved to its actual owner.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FComputed%20classification%20and%20treatment",
          "start_line": 916
        }
      ],
      "key": "computed_classification",
      "label": "Computed classification with C, J and O"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A change-span gate applying the closure condition owned by TICKETS §7 to declared obligations, using recorded evidence and results. It checks upstream sufficiency, downstream consistency with active upstream truth, and proof of active intended behaviour.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1042,
          "quote": "No gate closes while the affected active surfaces are internally\ninconsistent",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FConsistency%20gate",
          "start_line": 1022
        }
      ],
      "key": "consistency_gate",
      "label": "Consistency gate"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "SPEC_METHOD's sprint is a bounded execution-control surface under existing constitutional authority. It batches work, coordinates tickets or iteration entries, prices proof cost and forces close review. Deferred local compliance is escrow, while sprint close is forensic reconciliation rather than automatic approval.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1058,
          "quote": "A sprint is a bounded execution-control surface, not a new layer in the\nconstitutional chain",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FSprint%20execution%20boundary",
          "start_line": 1053
        }
      ],
      "key": "sprint_execution_boundary",
      "label": "Sprint execution boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Lifetime-based carrier selection: a durable ticket for identity across sessions, a sprint-local entry for traceability valuable only inside a batch, or a run/return record for work confined to one activation. TICKETS §1 owns the model and §5 the smallest-lawful-carrier selection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1107,
          "quote": "The carrier of a work item is selected for its required lifetime:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FCarriers",
          "start_line": 1098
        }
      ],
      "key": "work_carrier_selection",
      "label": "Work carrier selection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Four source-defined procedures: substantive intake; feature introduction/change; project bootstrap or requirement-surface repricing; and response to a real-use-case gap. They describe constitutional re-entry, forward derivation, design/implementation coordination, ambiguity recording and evidence production, without instantiating these operations here.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1178,
          "quote": "When any substantive intake arrives",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FProcedures",
          "start_line": 1111
        }
      ],
      "key": "method_procedures",
      "label": "Method procedures"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Substrate-neutral bounded context urn:stdo:bounded-context:ambiguity-governance under the selected complete basis. Goals narrow priority/work wave; intent direction/scope; product realisation shape/terms; requirements constitutional obligation; tenant or stack binding executable realisation class; design structural interpretation; implementation local detail. Major ambiguity materially affects architecture, stack, product boundary, execution/deployment admissibility, public contracts or downstream law; micro ambiguity is local choice within governed design.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1201,
          "quote": "This member owns the substrate-neutral ambiguity-governance bounded context",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
          "start_line": 1182
        }
      ],
      "key": "ambiguity_governance",
      "label": "Ambiguity governance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Lawful probabilistic processing, urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:lawful-probabilistic-processing, is bounded non-human processing permitted by declared policy to carry or resolve declared ambiguity. Human adjudication, urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication, is explicit human judgment admitted by authority to resolve ambiguity where policy or risk appetite requires it. Operation kind depends on admission and required authority, not merely the presence of a person.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1228,
          "quote": "That admission makes an operation\nhuman adjudication.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
          "start_line": 1216
        }
      ],
      "key": "ambiguity_processing_kinds",
      "label": "Probabilistic processing and human adjudication"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Recursive product lineage distinguishes substrate, development product, source project, release cut, product, install, artifact and product definition. A released product may build its successor, a dependent product or downstream artifacts. The product definition is the source project's present-tense surface, distinct from released artifact and builder. Immutable v<version>-rc.<n> identifies an exact point-in-time accepted-feature release cut; mutable v<version> discovers the highest-ordinal published immutable cut.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1275,
          "quote": "The only exact operative release identifier is the immutable release-cut\ntag `v<version>-rc.<n>`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F6.%20Lineage%20and%20release",
          "start_line": 1250
        }
      ],
      "key": "lineage_and_release",
      "label": "Lineage and release identities"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An authority-causality chain for reusable method changes: incident/review evidence → shared-method intake → one owning standard → consistency and independent review → human-authority acceptance → one complete released STDO version → explicit consumer selection → consumer-owned realisation and evidence. The chain does not prescribe intake workflow or artifact set.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1310,
          "quote": "The chain states authority causality. It prescribes no intake workflow and\nno artifact set",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F6.%20Lineage%20and%20release%2FShared%20method%20adoption%20and%20propagation%20%28STDO-UP-012%29",
          "start_line": 1293
        }
      ],
      "key": "shared_method_adoption",
      "label": "Shared method adoption and propagation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A constitutional migration for changes to load-bearing shared contracts, carriers, resolvers, providers, projection laws, closure laws or other interfaces on which multiple surfaces depend. It replaces authoritative core truth across producers, consumers and downstream effects; partial architecture split is not completion.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1340,
          "quote": "Core interface changes are not ordinary local patches.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FCore%20interface%20migration",
          "start_line": 1322
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_interface_migration",
      "label": "Core interface migration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Two lawful constitutional strategies: inside-out hard-break replacement on the same implementation line, proceeding from source carrier outward; and fundamental re-adoption for an intentional major rederivation on materially different runtime, carrier, type-system, substrate or realisation-tree basis, with prior implementation held sideways as reference. Bounded evolution is a rebuttable selection presumption, not a third strategy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1366,
          "quote": "Core interface and major implementation replacement work has two lawful\nconstitutional strategies",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line": 1344
        }
      ],
      "key": "constitutional_migration_strategies",
      "label": "Constitutional migration strategies"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An outcome's acceptance path is its full causal closure: every admitted entrypoint, producer, consumer, resolver, fallback, shared state surface, event, projection and proof surface capable of changing, interpreting or closing it. Singular authority covers this closure rather than one successful trace. Intermediate product-slice promotion and complete migration closure are distinct claims.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1404,
          "quote": "For this rule the affected acceptance path is the full causal closure of\nthe outcome:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line": 1388
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_slice_acceptance_path",
      "label": "Product-slice acceptance path"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Any shared contract or carrier materially governing runtime closure, reporting/status truth, topology/frame progression, proof/qualification outcome, event/projection semantics, identity/binding resolution or provider/resolver behaviour. A change capable of altering how multiple surfaces decide current truth is a core-interface change.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1420,
          "quote": "A core interface is any shared contract or carrier that materially governs",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FWhat%20counts%20as%20a%20core%20interface",
          "start_line": 1415
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_interface",
      "label": "Core interface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The explicit migration declaration and inventory, including old-path states remove, replace, re-authorize and temporary scaffolding.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1434,
          "quote": "Every old path is then classified as `remove`,\n`replace`, `re-authorize` or `temporary scaffolding`.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FRequired%20migration%20protocol",
          "start_line": 1424
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_protocol",
      "label": "Required migration protocol"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Compatibility aliases, fallback identity laws, bridge paths, old readers/writers and proxy or partial interfaces can preserve superseded authority. An explicit live-product compatibility feature is distinguished from current native truth.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1448,
          "quote": "The only lawful exception is an explicit compatibility\nfeature retained as part of the live product",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FBridge%20prohibition",
          "start_line": 1438
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_bridge_paths",
      "label": "Bridge paths and compatibility features"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Migration projections, reports, status views and topology views are downstream reflections of authoritative carriers, without a second closure authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1459,
          "quote": "Projections, reports, status views and topology views may reflect the\nauthoritative carrier; they do not become a second closure surface",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FProjection%20discipline",
          "start_line": 1452
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_projections",
      "label": "Migration projections"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Inside-out migration ordering from the new authoritative source carrier to downstream read models, with explicit ticket dependencies and an initially discovered best-guess interface family.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1476,
          "quote": "Inside-out hard-break migrations proceed from the new authoritative source\ncarrier outward",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FInside-out%20first%20sequencing",
          "start_line": 1463
        }
      ],
      "key": "inside_out_sequencing",
      "label": "Inside-out first sequencing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A migration wave is an ordered set of deliberate hard breaks over one interface family, not a mode licensing ambiguous or competing authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1485,
          "quote": "The migration wave is the ordered set of those breaks;",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FHard-break%20discipline",
          "start_line": 1480
        }
      ],
      "key": "hard_break_sequence",
      "label": "Hard-break sequence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Three distinct proof scopes: per-break rejection/fail-closed proof; bounded intermediate product-slice proof; and complete migration-closure proof after the migration wave.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1509,
          "quote": "Bounded product-slice\nproof may establish only the exact intermediate outcome permitted by the\npath-relative promotion rule",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FProof-last%20rule",
          "start_line": 1500
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_proof_scopes",
      "label": "Migration proof scopes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Completion across the declared affected migration scope, distinct from accepted intermediate slices. Retention is a justification relation for a superseded mechanism serving a distinct currently supported responsibility.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1537,
          "quote": "Retention bears the burden.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FClosure%20criterion",
          "start_line": 1513
        }
      ],
      "key": "migration_closure",
      "label": "Migration closure and retention"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A constitutional reset on a new branch, version line or methodology basis. Prior constitutional documents become source material; prior design/code may be references. Constitutional adoption states are adopted, superseded, deferred and orphaned. Implementation states are carry_across (materially same, explicitly re-adopted), redundant (unneeded), and rewrite (needed but rebuilt under new requirements/design). These adoption states differ from live requirement standing and from RELEASE §9 predecessor-claim dispositions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1609,
          "quote": "fundamental migration is controlled adoption, not passive preservation.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FFundamental%20migration",
          "start_line": 1541
        }
      ],
      "key": "fundamental_migration",
      "label": "Fundamental migration and adoption states"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Refactor and migration are described as a transformation wave over mutable implementation surfaces, with restricted temporary mixed state while in flight and one current operative surface across the affected scope when landed.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1634,
          "quote": "Refactor and migration should be understood as a transformation wave over\nmutable\nimplementation surfaces",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FTransformation%20wave",
          "start_line": 1613
        }
      ],
      "key": "transformation_wave",
      "label": "Transformation wave"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Source-defined application of ACTIVATION's step to agentic/probabilistic construction: accepted basis, bounded affected relations, selected computational relations, delegated execution/decision authority, construction/self-review, independent live-surface assessment where required, owner disposition, accepted checkpoint and next already-authorised action. Candidate availability, assessment and accept/local_repair/re_enter/reject disposition are separate results. Preservation, progress and completion are separate construction claims. Tenant binding supplies execution means and resource limits; configuration selects frames and return consumer.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1669,
          "quote": "a candidate that is available or\nassessed is not thereby accepted",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F8.%20Bounded%20execution%20in%20this%20method",
          "start_line": 1638
        }
      ],
      "key": "bounded_execution",
      "label": "Bounded execution"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Evidence for constitutional realisation claims: authority trace, realisation proof, negative proof where drift is plausible, and present-tense residual open pressure. Proof identity/adequacy, semantic/evidence/projection separation and assurance congruence/qualification belong to EVALUATION; verification layers, testing taxonomy, fixture/proof portability, test authority, scenario bundles, installed proof and significant-path coverage belong to ASSURANCE.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1727,
          "quote": "includes an authority trace, realisation proof, negative proof\nwhere drift is plausible, and a present-tense statement of the residual\nopen pressure",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F9.%20Evidence",
          "start_line": 1711
        }
      ],
      "key": "realisation_evidence",
      "label": "Realisation evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An ADR records a design decision boundary: the choice making acceptance criteria true. Its fields are Status (active/superseded/retired), Implements requirement identifiers, Derives from intent identifier or strategy, Supersedes prior ADR/doctrine, Superseded by successor when superseded, and Retained special case when earlier behaviour is intentionally retained. ADR files are constitutional sources; an optional REGISTRY.md is a potentially drifting read model.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1780,
          "quote": "ADRs are written per decision boundary, not per requirement file;",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F10.%20Design%20records",
          "start_line": 1731
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_record",
      "label": "Architectural decision record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "One independent, project-owned HOW realisation of a product's WHAT, located by its product-definition tenant registry. One WHAT has one or more independent tenants regardless of layout. A tenant owns its binding, realisation and evidence; tenant-local truth remains derivative until ratified in specification.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1840,
          "quote": "A build tenant is one independent, project-owned HOW realisation of a\n  product's WHAT.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F11.%20Build-tenant%20binding",
          "start_line": 1791
        }
      ],
      "key": "build_tenant",
      "label": "Build tenant"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The product WHAT's obligations, permitted outcomes and rules for refining and composing realisation work.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
